begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-008','[["Quality as conformance and fitness for use","A deliverable meets the contractor''s internal specification but fails the user''s operational need and agreed acceptance criteria.","Treat the deliverable as nonconforming and reassess both requirements and fitness for intended use.","Accept it because internal technical specifications are the only measure of quality.","Declare it high quality because it was expensive to produce.","Ask users to change their needs to fit the deliverable.","Project quality includes conformance to requirements and suitability for intended use, not merely technical completion."],["Quality planning","The team begins production without defining standards, metrics, responsibilities, assurance activities, control methods or acceptance criteria.","Develop a quality management plan aligned with scope, stakeholder requirements, regulation and organisational policy.","Rely on final inspection to determine quality after all work is complete.","Allow each supplier to choose its own undocumented quality standard.","Use the risk register as the only quality plan.","Quality planning defines applicable requirements, measures, roles, processes and evidence before delivery."],["Quality metrics and acceptance criteria","The requirement says a service must be reliable, but no uptime target, measurement period or exception rule is stated.","Define a measurable reliability metric and objective acceptance criteria.","Allow the client to decide informally whether reliability feels adequate.","Remove reliability from the scope because it is difficult to measure.","Measure quality by the number of staff assigned.","Clear metrics and acceptance criteria make quality requirements testable, monitorable and enforceable."],["Quality assurance versus quality control","Defects recur because the production process is unstable, yet management responds only by inspecting more finished outputs.","Strengthen quality assurance by improving the process while continuing appropriate quality control of outputs.","Eliminate process review and double final inspection only.","Accept recurring defects if they are detected before handover.","Assign all quality responsibility to the customer.","Quality assurance focuses on process capability and prevention; quality control measures outputs and identifies nonconformance."],["Quality audits","A team claims compliance with the quality plan, but there is no independent evidence that procedures are followed or effective.","Conduct a planned quality audit, document findings and track corrective actions.","Accept verbal assurance from the team as sufficient evidence.","Audit only after project closure when changes are no longer possible.","Use the budget variance report as the audit.","Quality audits examine whether processes comply with requirements and are effective, supporting corrective and improvement action."],["Continuous improvement and PDSA","A recurring handover problem is corrected temporarily each month but the process is never studied or standardised.","Use a structured improvement cycle such as Plan-Do-Study-Act to test, learn and institutionalise an effective change.","Repeat the same temporary fix indefinitely.","Change several variables at once without measuring results.","Wait for a major failure before improving the process.","PDSA supports controlled experimentation, learning and sustained process improvement."],["Inspection, testing and statistical sampling","A large batch cannot be inspected item by item, and the team proposes checking only the easiest units to access.","Design a representative statistical sampling plan based on risk, standards and required confidence.","Inspect only units that appear likely to pass.","Accept the batch without evidence because full inspection is impractical.","Select samples according to staff convenience with no documented method.","Representative, risk-based sampling provides more reliable evidence than convenience selection."],["Root-cause analysis","A defect is repeatedly repaired, but the underlying cause in the process remains unknown.","Use a root-cause technique such as the five whys or fishbone diagram and address the verified cause.","Continue correcting each defective output without investigating.","Blame the last person who touched the product.","Increase inspection frequency and assume the cause is resolved.","Corrective action should address the verified root cause, not only the visible symptom."],["Corrective and preventive action","A nonconformance has occurred and a similar failure is foreseeable in another workstream.","Implement corrective action for the existing cause and preventive or risk-reduction action for the potential recurrence elsewhere.","Use the terms corrective and preventive interchangeably without analysing cause.","Close the issue after repairing the single defective item.","Wait for the second workstream to fail before acting.","Corrective action removes causes of detected nonconformance; preventive action reduces causes of potential nonconformance."],["Seven basic quality tools","The team needs to distinguish the few defect categories causing most failures from many minor categories.","Use a Pareto chart supported by reliable defect data.","Use an organisation chart to rank defect causes.","Use a Gantt chart to calculate defect frequency.","Use a stakeholder matrix as the primary quality analysis.","A Pareto chart ranks categories by frequency or impact and helps focus improvement on the vital few."],["Quality standards and frameworks","A project adopts ISO 9001 terminology but has no controlled processes, evidence, leadership commitment or improvement system.","Implement the underlying quality-management principles and processes rather than treating certification language as a label.","Display the ISO name on reports and assume compliance.","Use ISO 9001 as a product inspection checklist only.","Ignore organisational context and customer requirements.","Quality standards require an effective management system, evidence and continual improvement, not superficial terminology."],["Regulatory and industry-specific quality","A healthcare project meets its internal quality target but ignores mandatory patient-safety and regulatory requirements.","Integrate applicable regulatory and industry standards into planning, assurance, control and acceptance.","Use internal targets because regulation is external to the project.","Address compliance only if an inspector visits.","Trade mandatory safety requirements for schedule savings.","Mandatory regulatory and industry standards are non-negotiable quality requirements and must be designed into delivery."],["Cost of Quality","Management cuts training, process design and testing to save money, after which rework, claims and reputational damage increase.","Evaluate the full cost of quality and invest appropriately in prevention and appraisal to reduce failure costs.","Continue cutting prevention because only direct production cost matters.","Classify rework and claims as unrelated project overhead.","Eliminate testing so defects are not formally recorded.","Cost of quality includes prevention, appraisal and internal and external failure costs; underinvestment upstream often increases total cost."],["Quality roles and quality culture","The project manager states that quality belongs only to the quality officer, and team members stop reporting defects.","Establish shared responsibility, leadership commitment, psychological safety and clear quality accountabilities.","Keep quality information within the quality department.","Reward teams for hiding defects to protect performance ratings.","Make customers solely responsible for discovering nonconformance.","Quality is built by the whole team, supported by leadership, clear roles and a culture that encourages early detection and learning."],["Quality dashboards and continuous learning","Reports show only the number of inspections completed and do not reveal defect trends, process capability, rework or corrective-action closure.","Use a balanced quality dashboard with meaningful indicators, trends, thresholds and lessons feeding future work.","Increase the inspection count target regardless of defect results.","Report only positive metrics to avoid concern.","Delete historical data after each reporting period.","Quality monitoring should reveal performance, trends, causes and action effectiveness and support organisational learning."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
